WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.680
The muscle builds its band without agrin.

2
00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:09.560
So why do mice lacking agrin end up with so few clusters?

3
00:00:10.733 --> 00:00:13.613
Something must be removing the clusters the muscle made.

4
00:00:14.617 --> 00:00:16.797
What could the nerve bring that removes clusters?

5
00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:29.630
A second signal. Acetylcholine, released at every terminal, disperses clusters that

6
00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:30.770
agrin does not protect.

7
00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:35.710
To test that, use mice whose nerves release no acetylcholine.

8
00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:38.090
They lack the enzyme that makes it.

9
00:00:38.983 --> 00:00:41.363
Their receptors still cluster under the nerve.

10
00:00:42.123 --> 00:00:44.583
So acetylcholine is not what starts clusters.

11
00:00:45.683 --> 00:00:48.763
And they keep more clusters, spread over a wider band.

12
00:00:49.717 --> 00:00:50.977
Now lay out a grid.

13
00:00:52.017 --> 00:00:53.197
Agrin present or absent.

14
00:00:54.237 --> 00:00:55.897
Acetylcholine present or absent.

15
00:00:56.950 --> 00:00:59.930
Normal mice have a narrow band under the terminals.

16
00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:03.870
Without agrin, early clusters form, then most are lost.

17
00:01:04.883 --> 00:01:08.723
Without acetylcholine, there are more clusters, over a wider band.

18
00:01:09.683 --> 00:01:11.143
Now predict the last cell.

19
00:01:12.183 --> 00:01:14.083
Remove both agrin and acetylcholine.

20
00:01:14.963 --> 00:01:16.103
What happens to the clusters?

21
00:01:22.633 --> 00:01:26.033
Clusters return, built by the muscle's own MuSK program.

22
00:01:26.853 --> 00:01:29.333
Two mouse studies reported this in 2005.

23
00:01:30.533 --> 00:01:34.273
Without acetylcholine, clusters persist even without agrin.

24
00:01:35.133 --> 00:01:39.673
So a major job of agrin in developing muscle is to protect clusters from

25
00:01:39.673 --> 00:01:40.673
acetylcholine.

26
00:01:41.567 --> 00:01:42.847
One puzzle remains.

27
00:01:43.767 --> 00:01:46.847
The cluster under the terminal gets the most acetylcholine.

28
00:01:47.707 --> 00:01:48.707
Why does it survive?

29
00:01:49.717 --> 00:01:52.617
In the working model, the two forces differ in reach.

30
00:01:53.717 --> 00:01:57.917
Agrin is anchored in the basal lamina, so its protection stays under the terminal.

31
00:01:58.967 --> 00:02:04.027
The dispersing effect of acetylcholine reaches clusters along the rest of the fibre.

32
00:02:04.927 --> 00:02:07.807
Away from the terminal, they lack agrin's strong protection.

33
00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:12.420
How that effect reaches distant clusters is not fully settled.

34
00:02:13.483 --> 00:02:15.503
The two forces meet at one question.

35
00:02:16.423 --> 00:02:17.423
Does a cluster stay?

36
00:02:18.303 --> 00:02:20.143
Agrin, acting through MuSK, holds it.

37
00:02:21.203 --> 00:02:22.883
Acetylcholine's pathway pulls it apart.

38
00:02:23.867 --> 00:02:27.727
Without agrin, Lrp4 is thought to give MuSK some activity.

39
00:02:28.507 --> 00:02:30.987
That would explain why a few small clusters remain.

40
00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:37.250
Raise MuSK enough another way, and synapses can form even in mice lacking agrin.

41
00:02:38.233 --> 00:02:42.033
This rule is for receptor clusters in developing mouse muscle.

42
00:02:43.093 --> 00:02:45.393
There, the muscle proposes; the nerve disposes.

43
00:02:46.517 --> 00:02:49.617
Takeaway: agrin protects the clusters beneath it.

44
00:02:50.417 --> 00:02:52.097
Acetylcholine disperses the rest.
